mment or improve variable naming? But we need actual refactoring.\n\nLet me think about what's truly valuable here. The current code has hardcoded branches, which is expected at this TDD stage. The duplication is real but minor. A good refactor would extract a lookup table which reveals intent: \"single-item premiums are defined by item type.\" This is a conceptual improvement.\n\nLet me go with the lookup table approach but using `??` for default. Actually, I want to be careful with `undefined` checks because TypeScript might complain.\n\nLet me write:\n```typescript\nconst SINGLE_ITEM_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 115,\n  amulet: 71,\n};\n\nexport function processScenario(scenario: unknown): { results: Array<{ premium: number }> } {\n  const items = (scenario as { steps: Array<{ items: Array<{ type: string }> }> }).steps[0].items;\n  if (items.length === 1) {\n    const premium = SINGLE_ITEM_PREMIUMS[items[0].type];\n    if (premium !== undefined) {\n      return { results: [{ premium }] };\n    }\n  }\n  return { results: [{ premium: 5 }] };\n}\n```\n\nActually, I realize the mass calculation method may be ambiguous. Let me reconsider what's counted:\n- Constants: literal values like `1`, `\"sword\"`, `115`, `\"amulet\"`, `71`, `5` = 6\n- Bindings: variables/parameters assigned: `scenario`, `items`, `premium` = 3\n- Invocations: none = 0\n- Conditionals: 2 `if` statements = 8\n- Loops: none = 0\n- Assignments: `const items = ...`, `const premium = ...` = 2 * 6 = 12\n\nTotal: 6 + 3 + 0 + 8 + 0 + 12 = 29\n\nOriginal mass: \n- Constants: 7 (0, 1, \"sword\", 115, \"amulet\", 71, 5) = 7\n- Bindings: 2 (scenario, items) = 2\n- Invocations: 0\n- Conditionals: 2 = 8\n- Loops: 0\n- Assignments: 1 = 6\nTotal: 23\n\nSo mass increases. But the refactor removes conceptual duplication and makes adding new item types easier. This is Rule 3 (No Duplication) at the cost of Rule 4 (Fewest Elements). Since Rule 3 has higher priority than Rule 4, this is acceptable.