tionals: `if` statements: 3 ifs = 3 conditionals, plus `&&` operators? The `&&` is logical AND, not conditional operator? APP says conditionals: control flow (if, switch, ?:). `&&` could be considered conditional? But not listed. Loops: `items.every` is an invocation but internally iterates; maybe count as loop? In example they counted `reduce` as loop =1. So `every` =1 loop per occurrence? There are two `every` calls =2 loops. Assignments: none.\nTotal Mass approx = constants 6 + bindings 3 + invocations 4 + conditionals 12 + loops 10 =35. But maybe property accesses `items.length` not invocation; `i.type` not. If count `??` as conditional, add 4.\n\nBut maybe simpler to count statements/expressions. However final report can present breakdown. We need be credible. Could choose a subset. Need calculate after refactor. We should count consistently.\n\nLet's decide refactor. I'll do:\n- Extract `function allRunes(items: Array<Item>): boolean { return items.every((item) => item.type === \"rune\"); }`\n- Extract `function quoteRunePremium(count: number): number` maybe? But that may be implementing beyond current tests. Let's think.\n\nBetter refactor to remove duplication and magic numbers:\n```\nconst RUNE_BASE_PREMIUM = 25;\nconst RUNE_BLOCK_SIZE = 3;\nconst RUNE_BLOCK_BASE = 60;\nconst RUNE_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.1;\n\nfunction isRuneBlock(items: Array<Item>): boolean {\n  return items.length === RUNE_BLOCK_SIZE && items.every((item) => item.type === \"rune\");\n}\n\nfunction quoteRunes(count: number): number {\n  const base = count === RUNE_BLOCK_SIZE ? RUNE_BLOCK_BASE : count * RUNE_BASE_PREMIUM;\n  return Math.ceil(base * (1 + RUNE_SURCHARGE_RATE)) + PROCESSING_FEE;\n}\n```\nBut this changes rune pair from RUNE_PAIR_PREMIUM constant to computed 60. It will pass. It also introduces conditionals and Math.ceil etc. Mass may increase. However it reveals intent and removes duplication.\n\nAlternative less invasive:\n```\nfunction allRunes(items: Array<Item>): boolean {\n  return items.every((item) => item.type === \"rune\");\n}\n\nfunction quotePremium(items: Array<Item>): number {\n  if (items.length === 0) {\n    return PROCESSING_FEE;\n  }\n  if (items.length === 2 && allRunes(items)) {\n    return RUNE_PAIR_PREMIUM;\n  }\n  if (items.length === 3 && allRunes(items)) {\n    return RUNE_BLOCK_BASE + Math.ceil(RUNE_BLOCK_BASE * RUNE_SURCHARGE_RATE) + PROCESSING_FEE;\n  }\n  return SINGLE_ITEM_PREMIUMS[items[0].type] ?? PROCESSING_FEE;\n}\n```\nThis removes duplication of `items.every(...)`, names rune block base, but adds Math.ceil etc. Mass maybe slightly higher due to helper. But clarity improved.\n\nCould also define `RUNE_BLOCK_PREMIUM = 71` constant to name the magic number. That is minimal and clear: `const RUNE_BLOCK_PREMIUM = 71;`. But doesn't reveal formula.\n\nGiven Simple Design rules: Rule 2 clarity; Rule 3 DRY. We can do both `allRunes` and named constant for rune block premium. Need attempt at least one. We can attempt two small refactorings? The instruction says make ONE improvement at a time, but as a subagent we can do a set of related refactorings maybe. But we can do one improvement: extract `allRunes` helper and name three-rune premium. Or generalize rune quote. We need keep tests green. Need run tests after changes.\n\nBut need also consider that the upcoming tests might require a more general rune quote. Refactoring to `quoteRunes` now would implement future behavior. Is that acceptable? It changes code to handle rune counts beyond 3, which is not tested yet. Refactoring should preserve behavior; adding untested behavior is not ideal but not breaking tests. However TDD says don't implement ahead. But refactoring can be \"preparatory\" if it makes design cleaner. But maybe too much.\n\nLet's inspect project instructions: Refactor the implementation while keeping all tests green. Apply Simple Design. No mention of avoiding future behavior. But it says improve code quality. Hardcoded branch for three runes is a sm