So bindings=7.\nInvocations: items.every (2x) =2, quotePremium(items) =1, processScenario function is declaration not invocation. (scenario as Scenario) is type assertion, not invocation. Array<Item> type constructor not runtime. Total invocations=3? But property access `.length` not invocation, `.type` not, `.steps[0].items` not. Conditionals: if statements 3 = 12. Loops: items.every internal iteration, count each =2*5=10. Assignments: const declarations count as assignments? APP says assignments: mutations (`x=5`,`count++`). Declarations with const are bindings and assignments? The example only counted assignments as mutations inside reduce? Let's treat const declarations as bindings, not assignments, unless reassignment. So assignments=0.\nTotal =13+7+6+12+10=48. But this seems high due to many constants.\n\nAfter refactor adding helper allRunes and constants RUNE_BLOCK_PREMIUM etc. Counting entire file might be more constants. But mass of quotePremium function maybe lower. We can measure just quotePremium region to show improvement. However adding helper may increase total file mass but improve per-function clarity. The instructions say compare code mass. We can choose a scope and be consistent.\n\nMaybe easier: Use a simple metric by counting tokens? But APP is specified. We need produce numbers. We can calculate mass for `quotePremium` and `allRunes` together (the rune-related logic). Let's define components for that region.\n\nBefore quotePremium body mass:\nConstants: 0,2,3,71,\"rune\",\"rune\" =6\nBindings: items, i, i =3\nInvocations: items.every (2) =4\nConditionals: 3 ifs =12\nLoops: items.every (2 loops) =10\nAssignments:0\nTotal=35.\n\nAfter refactor with allRunes helper and RUNE_BLOCK_PREMIUM constant:\nAdd constants: RUNE_BLOCK_PREMIUM=71 (literal 71), \"rune\" in helper =1 (since one helper). Actually replacing two \"rune\" in quotePremium with one in helper. So constants in quotePremium + helper: 0,2,3,\"rune\" (in helper), 71 =5 (down from 6).\nBindings: items (quotePremium param), item (helper param) =2 (down from 3).\nInvocations: allRunes(items) (2) + items.every (1 in helper) = 3 invocations *2 =6 (up from 4).\nConditionals: 3 ifs =12 (same).\nLoops: items.every in helper counted once per call; quotePremium calls allRunes twice -> each call iterates. But mass of source code loops: items.every expression =1 loop =5. The loop count is not dynamic; it's syntactic. So loop count remains 1 (down from 2) =5.\nAssignments:0.\nTotal =5+2+6+12+5=30. Lower. But we introduced new function declaration and constant, counted as bindings. allRunes adds an invocation and binding. But constants reduced.\n\nHowever if we also add a constant `RUNE_BLOCK_PREMIUM` and use it, the mass of constants still includes 71. If we replace `return 71` with `return RUNE_BLOCK_PREMIUM`, we add a binding but remove literal constant? In APP, constants are literal values; binding for constant name counts as binding. So replacing literal with named constant: constants -1, bindings +1. Net zero. But clarity improved.\n\nIf we compute rune block premium as `RUNE_BLOCK_BASE + RUNE_BLOCK_SURCHARGE + PROCESSING_FEE`, we add more constants/bindings and invocations. Might increase mass. But clarity maybe. To keep mass lower, just name constant `RUNE_BLOCK_PREMIUM`.\n\nBut the most important improvement is removing duplication of `items.every((i)=>i.type===\"rune\")`. We can also rename lambda parameter `i` to `item` for clarity. Good.\n\nLet's also consider `processScenario` uses `scenario as Scenario` and accesses first step. No refactor needed yet.\n\nPotential naming of helper: `allRunes` vs `areAllRunes`. Since it returns boolean, `areAllRunes` reads better. But `allRunes` also clear. Use `allRunes`.\n\nRefactor plan:\n1. Add helper `function allRunes(items: Array<Item>): boolean { return items.every((item) => item.type === \"rune\"); }`\n2. Add constant `const RUNE_BLOCK_PREMIUM = 71;`? But the value 71 also appears as amulet premium. There is a naming collision? Single amulet premium is 71, three runes premium also 71. The constant could be `THREE_RUNE_PREMIUM` or `RUNE_BLOCK_PREMIUM`. To avoid confusion with amulet, `THREE_RUNE_PREMIUM`. However `RUNE_BLOCK_PREMIUM` reveals block concept. Since spec says block base 60 + 10% + 5 fee, maybe `THREE_RUNE_BLOCK_PREMIUM`. Use `THREE_RUNE_PREMIUM`.\n3. Replace both rune checks with `allRunes(items)` and return constants.\n\nBut wait the value 71 is exactly amulet single premium; maybe it's not a coincidence. But tests: three runes expected 71. Hardcoded. Naming it `THREE_RUNE_PREMIUM` clarifies.\n\nAlso the `RUNE_PAIR_PREMIUM = 60` constant is used only for two runes; if we keep it, fine. We can rename to `TWO_RUNE_PREMIUM`? But spec says pair. Keep `RUNE_PAIR_PREMIUM`.\n\nMaybe extract a helper `quoteRunes(count)`? Let's consider if we want to attempt a more meaningful refactoring. The duplication is conceptual: two branches for runes both check all runes. Could collapse into one rune branch:\n```\nif (allRunes(items)) {\n  if (items.length === 2) return RUNE_PAIR_PREMIUM;\n  if (items.length === 3) return THREE_RUNE_PREMIUM;\n}\n```\nThis reduces duplication and conditionals? It adds nested if (still 2 conditionals inside) plus outer if =3 conditionals (same). But allRunes called once. Actually need both lengths. Could use switch. Mass maybe similar. But clearer? It groups rune logic. That's good.\n\nAlternatively:\n```\nfunction quoteRunes(count: number): number {\n  if (count === 2) return RUNE_PAIR_PREMIUM;\n  if (count === 3) return THREE_RUNE_PREMIUM;\n  return count * RUNE_BASE_PREMIUM; // not tested\n}\n```\nNot good due to untested branch.\n\nI think best minimal refactor: extract `allRunes` helper and use named constant for three-rune premium, grouping rune checks. Let's implement:\n```\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\nconst RUNE_PAIR_PREMIUM = 60;\nconst THREE_RUNE_PREMIUM = 71;\n\n...\n\nfunction allRunes(items: Array<Item>): boolean {\n  return items.every((item) => item.type === \"rune\");\n}\n\nfunction quotePremium(items: Array<Item>): number {\n  if (items.length === 0) {\n    return PROCESSING_FEE;\n  }\n  if (allRunes(items)) {\n    if (items.length === 2) return RUNE_PAIR_PREMIUM;\n    if (items.length === 3) return THREE_RUNE_PREMIUM;\n  }\n  return SINGLE_ITEM_PREMIUMS[items[0].type] ?? PROCESSING_FEE;\n}\n```\nThis removes duplicate `.every`, groups rune logic, names magic 71, renames lambda param to `item`. Tests should pass. However nested if may be slightly less flat but clearer grouping. We could also use a switch:\n```\nif (allRunes(items)) {\n  switch (items.length) {\n    case 2: return RUNE_PAIR_PREMIUM;\n    case 3: return THREE_RUNE_PREMIUM;\n  }\n}\n```\nSwitch is conditional (one switch + cases? APP conditionals: switch. Cases maybe constants). But more elements.\n\nMaybe keep flat but use helper:\n```\nif (items.length === 2 && allRunes(items)) return RUNE_PAIR_PREMIUM;\nif (items.length === 3 && allRunes(items)) return THREE_RUNE_PREMIUM;\n```\nThis only removes duplication of lambda but still calls allRunes twice (iterates twice). Could be okay. But grouping is better. However grouping means if items are all runes but length not 2 or 3, it falls through to single item premium (items[0].type rune => not in SINGLE_ITEM_PREMIUMS => processing fee). Currently for length 1 rune, first branch false, second false (length not 2), third false (length not 3), fallback to `SINGLE_ITEM_PREMIUMS[\"rune\"]` which is undefined -> processing fee 5. With grouping, allRunes true but lengths not 2/3, falls out and fallback same. Behavior preserved. For length 4 runes, same. Good.\n\nBut grouping with nested if increases nesting; Rule 2 clarity. Could extract `quoteRunePremium(count)` that returns premium or undefined, then:\n```\nconst runePremium = allRunes(items) ? quoteRunePremium(items.length) : undefined;\nif (runePremium !== undefined) return runePremium;\n```\nBut adds conditional. Hmm.\n\nSimpler