ch adds mass. Let's see:\n\nAfter extracting helper:\nNew bindings: count (helper param) = +1.\nNew invocations: *(count*COMPONENT_PREMIUM) - well, this moves from basePremium to helper. So no new invocation.\nNew conditional: the ?: in helper = +1 conditional. Wait, before we had `if/else` which is 1 conditional. With `?:` it's also 1 conditional. Same.\nBut we add a function invocation `componentPremium(count)` in basePremium = +1 invocation.\nAnd the helper function itself adds elements.\n\nActually the helper body:\n```ts\nfunction componentPremium(count: number): number {\n  return count === COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE ? COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM : count * COMPONENT_PREMIUM;\n}\n```\nBindings: count = 1.\nInvocations: componentPremium(call), ===, * = 3.\nConditionals: ?: = 1.\nNo loops, no assignments.\n\nMass added: +1 binding + 3*2 invocations + 1*4 conditional = +11.\nMass removed: the original if/else in basePremium. Before: conditional(1) + assignments(2) + invocation(*1) = 4+12+2 = 18. After replacing with `total += componentPremium(count)`: assignment(1) + invocation(componentPremium)(1) = 6+2 = 8. So mass change = +11 - 10 = +1. Slightly worse.\n\nBut clarity might improve. However, per Rule 2, clarity trumps mass. But the current if/else is already quite clear. I think we don't need this extraction.\n\nAnother option: Use `Map` instead of `Record` for componentsByType. But that adds complexity.\n\nAnother option: The `BASE_PREMIUMS[item.type] ?? 0` handles unknown item types by treating them as 0. This is probably not desired long-term (there's a todo for rejecting unknown item types), but tests pass. We could leave it.\n\nAnother option: `processScenario` hardcodes `steps[0].items` and ignores `op`. We could add a check for `op === \"quote\"` but that adds complexity without test demand. Rule 4: fewest elements.\n\nSo the main refactoring is using FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE.\n\nWait, is there an unused constant issue? FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE is defined but not used. That's a code smell. Using it is a clear improvement.\n\nAlso, should I check if tests pass before refactoring? Let me run tests first.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.bash:2","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"pnpm test","timeout":60}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/kimi-k2.7-code","usage":{"input":1966,"output":6284,"cacheRead":5696,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":6260,"totalTokens":13946,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784921626559,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-59d449a1-910f-4626-82f8-750eff83190a","responseModel":"moonshotai/kimi-k2.7-code"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"functions.bash:2","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n> tdd-experiment-run@ test /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-24_19-26-36_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_kimi-k2-7-no-thinking\n> vitest run\n\n\n RUN  v1.6.1 /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-24_19-26-36_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_kimi-k2-7-no-thinking\n\n ✓ src/claim-office.spec.ts  (35 tests | 27 skipped) 3ms\n\n Test Files  1 passed (1)\n      Tests  8 passed | 27 todo (35)\n   Start at  19:34:12\n   Duration  154ms (transform 26ms, setup 0ms, collect 24ms, tests 3ms, environment 0ms, prepare 42ms)\n\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784921652606},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good, tests pass. Now let me apply the refactoring: use FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE in quotePremium.\n\nI also want to consider if I should make any other improvements. Let me think again.\n\nActually, I notice that `basePremium` uses `if (count === COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE)`. For exact blocks of 3, it applies block premium. But what about 6 of the same type? The current code would treat them as `6 * 25 = 150`, not `2 * 60 = 120`. Is that correct? Looking at the spec from the todos, there's no test for 6 of the 