NCHANT_RATE : 0)\n  );\n}\n\nfunction calculateBaseAndSurcharges(items: Array<Item>): { policyBase: number; surchargeScaled: number } {\n  let policyBase = 0;\n  let surchargeScaled = 0;\n  const componentCounts: Record<string, number> = {};\n\n  for (const item of items) {\n    if (isComponent(item)) {\n      componentCounts[item.type] = (componentCounts[item.type] ?? 0) + 1;\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    const itemBase = BASE_PREMIUMS[item.type];\n    if (itemBase === undefined) {\n      throw new Error(`Unknown item type: ${item.type}`);\n    }\n    policyBase += itemBase;\n    surchargeScaled += itemBase * itemSurchargeRate(item);\n  }\n\n  for (const count of Object.values(componentCounts)) {\n    policyBase += count === COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE ? COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM : count * COMPONENT_PREMIUM;\n  }\n\n  return { policyBase, surchargeScaled };\n}\n\nfunction quotePremium(items: Array<Item>, customerYears: number, isFollowUp: boolean): number {\n  const { policyBase, surchargeScaled } = calculateBaseAndSurcharges(items);\n  const policyRate =\n    FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE +\n    (isFollowUp ? FOLLOW_UP_RATE : 0) +\n    (customerYears >= 2 ? LOYALTY_RATE : 0);\n\n  const totalScaled = policyBase * (SCALING + policyRate) + surchargeScaled + PROCESSING_FEE_SCALED;\n  return roundUpGold(totalScaled);\n}\n\nexport function processScenario(scenario: unknown): { results: Array<{ premium: number }> } {\n  const typed = scenario as Scenario;\n  const customerYears = typed.customer.yearsWithMHPCO;\n  const results: Array<{ premium: number }> = [];\n\n  for (let i = 0; i < typed.steps.length; i++) {\n    const step = typed.steps[i];\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      results.push({ premium: quotePremium(step.items ?? [], customerYears, i >= 1) });\n    }\n  }\n\n  return { results };\n}\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784921958067},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now claim tests. Need add claim processing to processScenario. Need maintain policies with items and remaining cap.\n\nTest 22: standard reimbursement regular steel sword enchant3 damage500 -> payout400. Need create policy via quote then claim. processScenario should return results with premium and payout/remainingCap.\n\nCurrently processScenario returns results array of {premium}. For claim steps, need return {payout, remainingCap}. We need change result type to array of objects. Tests use toEqual with objects. Type can be Array<Record<string,number>>. That's fine.\n\nNeed implement claim. Define policy state: array of { items, remainingCap }. Cap = 2 * insuranceSum. Insurance sum = sum item insurance values (main: sword1000, amulet600, staff800, potion400; component 250 each? The spec says components insured at 250G each. Block discount affects premium only. For insurance sum, components count individually 250 each. A block of 3 runes -> insurance sum 750? The example: sword + 3 runes insurance sum 1750 (=1000 + 3*250). Yes. So component insurance value per item 250.\n\nNeed track remaining cap. On claim, validate damages: each damage entry itemType must be in policy. Unknown -> error. Count per item type must not exceed policy count of that type. Negative amount -> error.\n\nClaim calculation per damage entry:\n- Determine item properties from policy items (find matching item? If multiple same type, how assign properties? The damages contain only itemType, not index. For multiple same type items, each entry separate damage with own deductible. But how determine enchantment/material? Since items are identical type but could differ (cursed etc). The spec doesn't clarify. Probably assume items of same type have same properties? Or for claim, we need look up item attributes. If multiple swords, maybe each damage entry corresponds to one insured sword. We can pair damages with policy items by type, consuming one item per damage. For determining enchantment/material, use the matched policy item. That handles multiple same type. For components, no enchantment/material.\n\nImplementation: For each damage entry:\n- Find a policy item with matching type that hasn't been used for this damage? Since each damage is separate, we need allocate. But for cap and validation, count damages per type <= policy count. For per-damage calculation, we can use any item of that type, but to be deterministic, iterate damages and assign the nth matching item. If counts equal, all assigned. For properties, use assigned item.\n- reimbursement base: if assigned item enchantment >=8 => damage*0.5; else if assigned item material === \"dragon\" => damage; else damage. Then subtract deductible 100. Ensure not negative? Spec doesn't mention negative payout; damage amounts > deductible in examples. If damage <100, payout 0? Probably 0 (full reimbursement minus deductible, max(0,damage-100)). Not tested. We'll max with 0.\n- Sum payouts across damages. Then apply cap: payout = min(sum, remainingCap). remainingCap -= payout. Round payout down (integer). Since damage and deductible integers, 50% can produce .5. Use Math.floor.\n\nPrecedence: high enchant >=8 overrides dragon material? The rule: if enchant>=8, 50% rule wins; else if dragon material full. So check enchant first.\n\nNeed apply cap after sum per claim. Payout rounded down. Since sum may be .5, floor. For cap, if sum > remainingCap, payout = remainingCap (integer). But remainingCap integer. Good.\n\nTest 22: policy sword enchant3 material steel, damage500. reimbursement full =500, deductible 100 =>400. cap 2000. payout400, remaining 1600.\n\nLet's implement claim handling. Also update result type. Need adjust existing tests expecting only premiums for scenarios with only quote. Adding claim results won't affect them.\n\nTest 22 activation.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.edit:104","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"src/claim-office.spec.ts","edits":[{"oldText":"  it.todo(\"should pay out 400 G for damage 500 G to a regular steel s